    <!-- Section 1: Breakpoints -->
    <section id="breakpoints" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M11 5H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v11a2 2 0 002 2h11a2 2 0 002-2v-5m-1.414-9.414a2 2 0 112.828 2.828L11.828 15H9v-2.828l8.586-8.586z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Breakpoints
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Use the <code class="text-amber-400">debug()</code> function to stop the request where it stands and answer with the interactive debug page, holding a snapshot of the locals at that line. This is useful for inspecting variable state and understanding program flow.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Setting Breakpoints</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">def process_user(user_id: Int) -> Hash
  user = database.find(user_id)
  # Set a breakpoint to inspect the user object
  debug()
  if (user == null)
    return {"error": "User not found"}
  end

  profile = enrich_profile(user)
  # Another breakpoint to see enriched data
  debug()
  profile
end</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Why Use Breakpoints?</h3>
        <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Inspect State</span>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Examine variable values and data structures at any point in your code</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Trace Execution</span>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Follow the program flow step by step to understand logic</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Evaluate Code</span>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Run arbitrary soli code to test hypotheses or calculate values</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-orange-500/20 text-orange-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Debug Errors</span>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Automatically triggered on errors to help diagnose issues</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20 p-4 mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-blue-400 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
                </svg>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-blue-400 font-semibold mb-1">A stop, not a pause</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-300">
                        The request unwinds at <code class="text-amber-400">debug()</code> and cannot be resumed or stepped; the REPL runs against the snapshot, not the live frame. Only <code class="text-amber-400">debug()</code> on a line of its own is a breakpoint &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">x = debug()</code> just stores a marker value. The VM does not compile <code class="text-amber-400">debug()</code>: a handler that calls it runs on the tree-walker instead, with the same result.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 mt-2">
                        <strong class="text-white">Remove it before you deploy.</strong> Outside <code>--dev</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">debug()</code> is not ignored: the request fails and the visitor gets the generic production error page with status <code>500</code> (the breakpoint is kept out of the error log). Under <code>--dev</code> the debug page answers <code>200</code>.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 mt-2">
                        This builtin was previously named <code class="text-amber-400">break()</code>. It was renamed because <code class="text-amber-400">break</code> is now a loop keyword — see <a href="/docs/language/control-flow#kw-break" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300 underline">Control Flow</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Step debugger -->
    <section id="step-debugger" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Step debugger (<code>soli dap</code>)</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">soli dap</code> is a <a href="https://microsoft.github.io/debug-adapter-protocol/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Debug Adapter Protocol</a> server on stdin/stdout. An editor that speaks DAP launches a script through it and can then stop, step and look inside it while it runs &mdash; a real pause, unlike the <code class="text-cyan-400">debug()</code> page.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Breakpoints</strong> on any line of the script or of a file it imports.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Step over, into and out</strong>, continue, and pause a running program.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">The call stack</strong>, with each frame&rsquo;s line, and for each frame its <strong class="text-white">Locals</strong> and the program&rsquo;s <strong class="text-white">Globals</strong> (builtins left out). Arrays, hashes and objects expand; a name that looks like a secret shows <code class="text-cyan-400">[REDACTED]</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Evaluate</strong> in the paused frame, from the debug console or on hover. It runs real code in that frame&rsquo;s scope, so <code class="text-cyan-400">total = 0</code> changes the variable.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white"><code>debug()</code> is a breakpoint</strong> while a debugger is attached: the program stops there and resumes after it.</li>
            <li>What the program prints arrives in the editor&rsquo;s debug console.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">The launch configuration takes <code class="text-cyan-400">program</code> (the <code>.sl</code> script, required), <code class="text-cyan-400">cwd</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">stopOnEntry</code>.</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Neovim (nvim-dap)</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6"><div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-lua text-sm">local dap = require("dap")
dap.adapters.soli = { type = "executable", command = "soli", args = { "dap" } }
dap.configurations.soli = {
  { type = "soli", request = "launch", name = "Debug this file",
    program = "${file}", cwd = "${workspaceFolder}" },
}</code></pre>
        </div></div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Helix (<code>languages.toml</code>)</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6"><div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-toml text-sm">[language.debugger]
name = "soli"
transport = "stdio"
command = "soli"
args = ["dap"]

[[language.debugger.templates]]
name = "script"
request = "launch"
completion = [{ name = "script", completion = "filename" }]
args = { program = "{0}" }</code></pre>
        </div></div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-amber-500/5 border border-amber-500/20 p-5">
            <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-2"><strong class="text-white">For now:</strong></p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 text-sm space-y-1">
                <li>It runs scripts (<code>soli script.sl</code>). Attaching to <code>soli serve</code> and debugging a spec come next.</li>
                <li>The program runs on the tree-walker, without native kernels, whatever engine <code>soli script.sl</code> would pick: that is the engine a debugger can stop.</li>
                <li>An outer frame&rsquo;s Locals are as they were at its last statement, the call it is waiting on.</li>
                <li>No conditional breakpoints or logpoints yet, and the VS Code extension does not declare the debugger yet: use an editor above, or any DAP client.</li>
                <li>On Windows the program&rsquo;s output is not captured, and can disturb the protocol.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: Debug Page -->
    <section id="debug-page" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-orange-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-orange-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9.75 17L9 20l-1 1h8l-1-1-.75-3M3 13h18M5 17h14a2 2 0 002-2V5a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Debug Page
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            When a breakpoint is hit or an error occurs, soli displays a comprehensive debug page with multiple panels for investigation.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Debug Page Features</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Interactive REPL - Execute soli code in breakpoint context
# Stack Trace - Navigate through call stack frames
# Source Viewer - View source code with line highlighting
# Request Inspector - Examine request params, query, headers, body
# Environment Info - View time, method, path of the request</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Stack Trace Navigation</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Click on any stack frame to view the source code at that location. The source viewer shows the relevant code with the error line highlighted.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-text text-sm">Stack Trace:
 0. process_user at ./src/users.sl:15
 1. handle_request at ./src/routes.sl:42
 2. main at ./src/main.sl:8

Click on any frame to see the source code.</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Source Code Viewer</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The source viewer displays code around the breakpoint or error location with:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Line numbers for easy reference</li>
            <li>Highlighted error/breakpoint line</li>
            <li>Context lines above and below</li>
            <li>Syntax highlighting</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Interactive REPL -->
    <section id="interactive-repl" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-emerald-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8 9l3 3-3 3m5 0h3M5 20h14a2 2 0 002-2V6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Interactive REPL
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            The debug page includes an interactive REPL that runs in the context of your breakpoint. You can execute any soli code to inspect and manipulate data.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 p-4 mb-6">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-300">
                The REPL endpoint is token-protected and accepts loopback clients by default. If your <code class="text-amber-300">*.test</code> domains point to a trusted local server on another machine, start dev mode with <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_DEV_REPL_ALLOW_REMOTE=1</code> <em>and</em> pin the token to a secret you control via <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_DEV_REPL_SECRET=&lt;long-random-string&gt;</code>. The server refuses to start in remote-allowed mode without the secret (SEC-051) so the credential is never embedded in HTML error pages.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm mt-3">
                <strong class="text-white">Local <code>Host</code> only.</strong> The REPL token, the dev-bar diagnostics, <code class="text-amber-300">/__dev/*</code>, the inbox and request replay answer only when the request's <code>Host</code> is local &mdash; <code>localhost</code>, <code>*.localhost</code>, an IP literal, or a host listed in <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_APP_HOSTS</code> &mdash; so a DNS-rebinding page cannot drive them through your browser. A name such as <code>myapp.test</code> or <code>mymac.local</code> must be added to <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_APP_HOSTS</code>; until it is, those endpoints 404 and error pages carry no REPL token. The inbox <em>clear</em> and <em>replay</em> POSTs also require a same-origin <code>Origin</code>/<code>Referer</code>, and the jobs dashboard is credential-free only from a loopback peer with a local <code>Host</code>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Available Variables</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Request object - access all request data
req              # Full request object
req["params"]    # Route parameters (e.g., {id: "123"})
req["query"]     # Query string parameters
req["body"]      # Request body (POST/PUT data)
req["headers"]   # HTTP headers

# Session
session          # Session data

# Breakpoint context
breakpoint_env   # Local variables at breakpoint</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">REPL Features</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Use @ to reference the last result
req["params"]["id"]  # Returns: "123"
@ + 100              # Returns: 223 (uses last result)

# Navigation with arrow keys
# Up/Down: Browse command history

# Execute any valid soli code
print("Debug output")user["name"];        # Inspect variable
config["database"];  # View configuration</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Quick Inspect Buttons</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Click the quick inspect buttons to instantly view common data:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Available quick inspect buttons:
req        # View full request object
params     # View route parameters
query      # View query string
body       # View request body
session    # View session data
headers    # View HTTP headers</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: AQL Query Log -->
    <section id="aql-query-log" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-cyan-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-cyan-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4 7v10c0 2.21 3.582 4 8 4s8-1.79 8-4V7M4 7c0 2.21 3.582 4 8 4s8-1.79 8-4M4 7c0-2.21 3.582-4 8-4s8 1.79 8 4m0 5c0 2.21-3.582 4-8 4s-8-1.79-8-4" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            AQL Query Log
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            When the server runs with <code class="text-cyan-400">--dev</code>, every query a request executes through the Model layer is captured into a per-request stack. The <code class="text-cyan-400">dev_queries()</code> builtin returns that stack, so you can render a debug bar showing every query, its bind variables, and how long it took.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            The query, HTTP and KV logs (also kept in production when <code class="text-cyan-400">SOLI_LOG</code> asks for them) hold at most <strong class="text-white">10&nbsp;000 entries</strong> per request, WebSocket/LiveView event or background job, and are reset at the start of each WebSocket event and each job.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-cyan-500/10 border border-cyan-500/20 mb-6">
            <p class="text-cyan-200 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-white">Every backend is covered.</strong> On SoliDB the entries
                are AQL; on the SQL adapters (<code>postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>,
                <code>sqlite</code>) they are the SQL actually sent, with binds numbered the way
                <code>$1</code> / <code>?</code> appear in the statement. The dev bar's DB panel and
                timings, the N+1 badge, <code class="text-cyan-400">assert_no_n_plus_one</code>, and
                <code class="text-cyan-400">soli test --fail-on-n1</code> therefore behave the same on
                all of them. Bind values over 200 characters are truncated (with their real length
                noted) so a large document cannot flood the panel.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Return Shape</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code class="text-cyan-400">dev_queries()</code> returns an <code>Array&lt;Hash&gt;</code> with these keys per entry:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Key</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Type</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">query</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">String</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">The AQL sent to SoliDB</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">bind_vars</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Hash | null</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Bind variables, or <code>null</code> if none</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">duration_ms</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Float</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Wall-clock time in milliseconds</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Controller Example</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">app/controllers/users_controller.sl</label>
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">def index
  users = User.where("doc.active == true").all
  posts = Post.includes("author").all

  # dev_queries() returns [] in production, populated under --dev
  render("users/index", {
    "users":   users,
    "posts":   posts,
    "queries": dev_queries()
  })
end</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Debug Bar Partial</h3>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">app/views/shared/_dev_bar.html.erb</label>
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-erb text-sm">&lt;% if queries.length &gt; 0 %&gt;
  &lt;div class="dev-bar"&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;&lt;%= queries.length %&gt; AQL queries&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;ol&gt;
      &lt;% for q in queries %&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;
          &lt;code&gt;&lt;%= h(q["query"]) %&gt;&lt;/code&gt;
          &lt;% if q["bind_vars"] != null %&gt;
            &lt;small&gt;binds: &lt;%= h(json_stringify(q["bind_vars"])) %&gt;&lt;/small&gt;
          &lt;% end %&gt;
          &lt;span&gt;&lt;%= q["duration_ms"] %&gt; ms&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;% end %&gt;
    &lt;/ol&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Coverage</h3>
        <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/20">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Logged</span>
                <ul class="text-sm text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
                    <li>All <code>Model</code> operations (<code>all</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>destroy</code>, <code>count</code>)</li>
                    <li>Eager-loaded <code>includes</code> and soft-delete scopes</li>
                    <li>Validation lookups (<code>uniqueness</code>)</li>
                    <li>HABTM join-table operations</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-red-500/10 border border-red-500/20">
                <span class="inline-block px-2 py-1 rounded bg-red-500/20 text-red-400 text-xs font-bold font-mono mb-2">Not Logged (v1)</span>
                <ul class="text-sm text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
                    <li>Direct <code>Solidb(host, db).query(...)</code> calls</li>
                    <li>Mocked queries from <code>register_query_mock</code> (short-circuit before the executor)</li>
                    <li>Internal session-storage queries via <code>SoliDBClient</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20 p-4 mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-blue-400 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
                </svg>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-blue-400 font-semibold mb-1">Zero Production Cost</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-300">
                        In production, the executor never calls into the logger — the gate is a single relaxed atomic load. <code class="text-cyan-400">dev_queries()</code> always returns <code>[]</code> there, so it's safe to leave the debug bar partial in your layout unconditionally.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4b: Dev Bar Flamegraph -->
    <section id="dev-bar-flamegraph" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-orange-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-orange-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M17.657 18.657A8 8 0 016.343 7.343S7 9 9 10c0-2 .5-5 2.986-7C14 5 16.09 5.24 17 7.341 18.1 9.5 18.1 11.5 17 13c-1.5 2-3 1-3-1 0-1 1-2 1-2-2 0-3 1-3 3 0 2 1 4 3 5z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Per-request Flamegraph
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Click <code class="text-orange-400">flame</code> in the dev bar to expand a hierarchical flamegraph of every span captured during the request — middleware, before/after actions, controller dispatch, view, partials, every Soli function call, plus DB and HTTP. Hover any rectangle for an exact duration; click to zoom in; double-click to reset.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">What gets captured</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Spans are emitted at well-known framework wrap points and at every interpreter-level function call. Color encodes the kind:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Kind</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Color</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Source</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-amber-300">middleware</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">amber</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Each scoped or global middleware invocation</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-amber-200">before_action / after_action</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">dim amber</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Per controller hook fire</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-cyan-300">action</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">cyan</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Top-level handler dispatch (e.g. <code>posts#show</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-300">view / partial</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">green</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Each <code>render(...)</code> / <code>render_partial(...)</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-orange-300">db</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">purple</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Each AQL query (name = first 80 chars)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-orange-300">http</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">pink</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Each outbound <code>HTTP.*</code> call</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-gray-400">fn</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">slate</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Every Soli function call, on the VM and the tree-walker alike</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-orange-400">fn</code> spans come from both engines: an action the VM runs shows its own functions, methods and blocks, as a tree-walked one does. A typed numeric function compiled to a native kernel opens no frame, so its time stays in its caller's span.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Export to Perfetto / chrome://tracing</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The flame panel includes a <code class="text-cyan-400">⬇ trace.json</code> link that downloads the same data as Chrome Trace Event Format JSON. Drop the file into <a href="https://ui.perfetto.dev/" class="text-cyan-400 hover:underline">ui.perfetto.dev</a> or open <code>chrome://tracing</code> for full timeline navigation, search, and aggregation across spans.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Large requests</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A request that walks a few hundred records produces thousands of spans, and drawing every one made the panel itself the slowest thing on the page (3.4&nbsp;MB of markup on a real dashboard). The chart therefore draws the <strong class="text-white">300 heaviest spans</strong> &mdash; chosen by duration, not by order, so the expensive ones are never the ones cut &mdash; and the header says so (<code class="text-cyan-400">showing 300 heaviest</code>). The span count in the header stays exact. Set <code class="text-cyan-400">SOLI_DEV_FLAME_MAX</code> to change the bound, or <code class="text-cyan-400">SOLI_DEV_FLAME_MAX=0</code> to draw everything.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code class="text-cyan-400">trace.json</code> is never truncated: it is the artefact you load into a profiler. Up to 64&nbsp;KB it is inlined in the link; above that the link points at <code class="text-cyan-400">/__solidev/trace/&lt;request-id&gt;</code>, a dev-only endpoint that serves the complete trace from the same ring buffer the requests panel reads (404 once the request has aged out).
        </p>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <p class="text-amber-300 text-sm">
                <strong>Production-safe.</strong> The flamegraph capture is gated on <code>--dev</code>: every span hook short-circuits on a thread-local enable flag, so production servers don't pay the cost. The panel is only injected into <code>text/html</code> responses, never into JSON or other content types.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/20">
            <p class="text-sky-300 text-sm">
                <strong>HTMx-aware.</strong> Soli skips dev bar injection on responses to requests carrying the <code>HX-Request: true</code> header. HTMx swaps a fragment into a page that already shows the dev bar, so re-injecting the bar into the fragment would stack a duplicate at the bottom of the page on every swap.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Reading the chart</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The X-axis is request time in microseconds (0 = request start). Y-axis is stack depth: parents are above their children. A wide rectangle near the top is a slow phase; a wide rectangle deep in the chart is a slow function. Look for:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400">
            <li>A view rectangle that spans most of the request → <code>render</code> dominates; check the partial breakdown row in the render panel.</li>
            <li>Many narrow purple rectangles at the same depth → likely an N+1; the AQL panel will flag it.</li>
            <li>A very wide cyan <code>action</code> with thin children → time is spent in user code outside the framework hooks; turn on the <code>fn</code> spans (always on under <code>--dev</code>) and zoom in to find the hot function.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4b2: Profiling -->
    <section id="profiling" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-rose-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-rose-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 19v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2zm0 0V9a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v10m-6 0a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2h-2a2 2 0 01-2-2z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Profiling Scripts &amp; Tests
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-rose-400">--profile</code> runs a script, <code>-e</code> code or a test suite under a sampling profiler and prints the hottest functions when it ends.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli report.sl --profile
soli test --profile
soli test tests/models --profile --profile-out models.json</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="text-sm">Profile: 1840 ms sampled over 1843 ms wall time, every 1000 µs
   self ms  self%   total ms total%  function
    1610.0  87.5%     1620.0  88.0%  score (report.sl:12)
     150.0   8.2%     1790.0  97.3%  rank (report.sl:30)
      60.0   3.3%     1840.0 100.0%  &lt;main&gt;
Profile written to soli-profile.folded</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Flag</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Meaning</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-rose-300">--profile</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Turn the profiler on (<code>--profile=cpu</code> is the same)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-rose-300">--profile-out FILE</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Where to write the profile (default <code>soli-profile.folded</code>); implies <code>--profile</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 font-mono text-rose-300">--profile-hz N</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Samples per second, 1 to 10&nbsp;000 (default 1000); implies <code>--profile</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            The file is <strong class="text-white">folded stacks</strong> &mdash; one <code>&lt;main&gt;;outer (app.sl:3);inner (app.sl:9) 42</code> line per distinct stack &mdash; unless its name ends in <code>.json</code>, which writes a <strong class="text-white">speedscope</strong> profile. Load either into <a href="https://www.speedscope.app" class="text-rose-400 hover:underline">speedscope.app</a>, or feed the folded file to <code>flamegraph.pl</code> or <code>inferno-flamegraph</code> for an SVG. On a script the flags may come before or after its name (<code>soli app.sl --profile</code>), up to the first argument that belongs to the script.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Reading a profile</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Wall time, both engines.</strong> A sampler thread ticks <code>--profile-hz</code> times a second; the engine charges the ticks to the Soli stack it is in at its next function entry or exit, tree-walker statement or VM loop check. A script waiting on the database or the network shows that wait, charged to the function that made the call.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Safepoint bias.</strong> Time lands where the engine next checks, not at the instant the tick fired, so a very short function can absorb the time of the native call just before it returns. Totals are right; single-line precision is not the point.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">What has a frame.</strong> Functions, methods, constructors, lambdas and blocks. A typed numeric function compiled to a native kernel runs without a frame and is charged to its caller. <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is the top level of the script.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white"><code>soli test</code>.</strong> Every spec file's top level is its own <code>&lt;main&gt; (file)</code> root, and the specs' worker threads are added together, so the total can exceed the wall time. Request specs run their controllers in a separate test-server process, which is not profiled. <code>--profile</code> cannot be combined with <code>--watch</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <p class="text-amber-300 text-sm">
                <strong>No cost when off.</strong> Without <code>--profile</code> the hooks cost one relaxed atomic load per call and per statement, and <code>soli serve</code> never turns them on.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4c: Page Requests -->
    <section id="dev-bar-requests" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-sky-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-sky-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Page Requests
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Every response the dev server sends carries an <code class="text-sky-400">X-Soli-Route</code> header naming the route that handled it (e.g. <code class="text-orange-400">users#show</code>). The dev bar header shows the current page's route right after the URL, and <strong>clicking the URL</strong> expands a <em>Requests</em> panel listing every route the page touched — the main page plus each XHR / <code>fetch</code> / HTMx call it fired afterwards.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">How it works</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The main page's row is rendered server-side. For everything after that, the dev bar patches <code class="text-cyan-400">fetch</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">XMLHttpRequest</code> once, reads <code>X-Soli-Route</code> off each response, and appends a row (method, URL, route, status, duration). Each row's <strong>duration is the server-side app render time</strong> (from the <code>X-Soli-Render-Us</code> header) — the real time spent in your app, not the client round-trip (which folds in queue + network + transfer). Hover the duration to see the round-trip too. The count next to the URL updates as calls come in. HTMx uses XHR/fetch under the hood, so its swaps are captured too — and because a swap doesn't reload the page, the list keeps growing across swaps.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The dev bar isn't re-injected on an HTMx swap (that would stack a second bar), so the header follows HTMx <strong>navigations</strong> instead: when HTMx pushes the URL (<code>hx-push-url</code> / <code>hx-boost</code>), the header's route and render time update to that request. Background or widget HTMx requests that don't change the URL are added to the panel but leave the header alone.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Inspect any request</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <strong>Click a row</strong> to retarget the <code class="text-orange-400">db</code>, <code class="text-orange-400">http</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">kv</code>, and <code class="text-orange-400">flame</code> panels (and their header badges) to that request — so you can open the flamegraph or query list for a specific XHR call, not just the main page. Every dev response also carries an <code class="text-sky-400">X-Soli-Request-Id</code>; the bar stores each request's snapshot server-side (a small ring buffer of the most recent requests) and the row-click fetches <code class="text-cyan-400">/__solidev/request/:id</code> to re-render its panels in place. The selected row is highlighted; row 0 is always the page itself.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Replay a request</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Each row carries a <span class="text-gray-300">&#8635;</span> button. Click it to <strong>replay that request server-side</strong>: the dev server re-dispatches the captured request (same method, path, query, headers, and body) through the real worker path, so the handler runs again — handy for reproducing a bug without re-driving the UI. The bar then retargets its panels to the replay's fresh <code class="text-sky-400">X-Soli-Request-Id</code>, so you see the new run's queries, HTTP calls, and flamegraph immediately. Replayed responses are tagged <code class="text-sky-400">X-Soli-Replay: 1</code>.
        </p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Dev tools menu</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The <code class="text-orange-400">tools</code> button at the right end of the bar opens the dev-only galleries, each in a new tab so the page you're inspecting stays put: the <a href="#mail-inbox" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">mail inbox</a> (<code class="text-cyan-400">/__soli/inbox</code>), the <a href="/docs/builtins/mailer#previewing" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">mailer previews</a> (<code class="text-cyan-400">/__soli/mailers</code>), and the <a href="/docs/core-concepts/views#component-catalog" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">component catalog</a> (<code class="text-cyan-400">/__soli/components</code>). The button carries the inbox's message count, so mail the app sent while you were clicking around announces itself without you going looking for it.
        </p>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-rose-500/10 border border-rose-500/20">
            <p class="text-rose-300 text-sm">
                <strong>Replaying re-runs side effects.</strong> A replayed <code>POST</code> / <code>PATCH</code> / <code>DELETE</code> re-executes its mutation (a second insert, a second charge, …), exactly as if the client had resubmitted. The per-form CSRF token check is skipped for replays (the session token may have rotated since capture), and multipart uploads aren't re-parsed in this version — the raw body is re-sent but file fields arrive empty. Replay only exists under <code>--dev</code>: the capture store is empty in production and the endpoint isn't mounted.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <p class="text-amber-300 text-sm">
                <strong>Production-safe.</strong> The route is recorded behind the same <code>--dev</code> thread-local gate as the other panels, and the <code>X-Soli-Route</code> header is only emitted in dev mode. In production no header is sent and no dev bar (or its script) is injected, so there is zero runtime cost.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/20">
            <p class="text-sky-300 text-sm">
                <strong>Scope &amp; caveats.</strong> Only <strong>same-origin</strong> requests that hit a Soli route (i.e. carry <code>X-Soli-Route</code>) become rows — static assets, cross-origin fetches, and 404s are ignored. The list <strong>resets on each full page load</strong> (it reflects the current page). Only the most recent requests are kept for inspection, so a long-idle row may report it has aged out. WebSocket, SSE, and <code>sendBeacon</code> traffic is not captured, and a request fired before the dev bar's script runs may be missed.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- soli agent -->
    <section id="soli-agent" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-violet-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-violet-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8 9l3 3-3 3m5 0h3M5 20h14a2 2 0 002-2V6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Attaching a Coding Agent (<code>soli agent</code>)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-cyan-400">soli agent</code> lets a coding agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI, any MCP client) read what the dev bar shows for the running <code>soli serve --dev</code>: the recent requests, each one's queries with binds and durations, the N+1 alert, and the open error groups. It can also replay a request, run one spec, and use the dev REPL. The agent edits files itself; <code>soli agent</code> writes nothing in the app.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            It is an <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">MCP</a> server on stdin/stdout: the client starts it, and it opens no port. Declare it from the app root &mdash; for Claude Code, a <code>.mcp.json</code> next to <code>soli.toml</code>:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "mcpServers": {
    "soli": { "command": "soli", "args": ["agent"] }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Start <code>soli serve . --dev</code> first. When it binds, the dev server writes <code class="text-amber-400">.soli/dev-server.json</code> (pid, port, app root and the dev REPL token, mode <code>0600</code>) and removes it on shutdown; <code>soli new</code> gitignores it. <code>soli agent</code> reads that file from the current directory and exits with <code>dev server is not running</code> when it is missing or its process is gone &mdash; it never scans ports. It then calls the dev server on <code>127.0.0.1</code> through the same <code>/__solidev/*</code> and <code>/__dev/repl</code> endpoints the dev bar uses, so the same gates apply: loopback peer, local <code>Host</code>, same-origin POST, REPL token.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Tool</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">What it returns</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">requests</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">The last 64 requests, newest first: id, method, path, status, <code>elapsed_us</code>, route, query count</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">request</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">One request: phase, middleware and template timings, every query (statement, binds, duration), outgoing HTTP and KV calls, and <code>n_plus_one</code> when the dev bar flags one</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">replay</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Re-runs a request like the &#8635; button and returns the new request id and status. Anything but <code>GET</code>/<code>HEAD</code> needs <code>confirm: true</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">errors</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Open <a href="/docs/development-tools/observability#error-tracking" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">error groups</a> with their latest sample: message, stack, a <code>curl</code> line, locals &mdash; redacted when stored</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">graph</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400"><code>soli graph query --json</code> for a question (needs <code>soli graph build</code> and SolidB)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">test</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400"><code>soli test &lt;path&gt;</code> for one spec file or directory, never the whole suite; failures only, 120&nbsp;s timeout, at most 20 failures / 8&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-cyan-400 font-mono">repl</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Runs Soli code inside the dev process &mdash; models, config, database. Pass <code>repl_session_id</code> back to keep variables</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The JSON behind <code>requests</code>, <code>request</code> and <code>errors</code> is served at <code class="text-cyan-400">GET /__solidev/requests</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">GET /__solidev/requests/:id</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">GET /__solidev/errors</code>, dev-only like the rest of <code>/__solidev/</code>. It carries no request headers, cookies or bodies: only what the dev bar and the error page already show.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A typical loop: <code>requests</code> shows <code>GET /orders</code> at 400&nbsp;ms with 53 queries; <code>request</code> shows 50 of them are the same <code>customers</code> lookup; the agent adds an <code>includes(...)</code>; <code>replay</code> of the same id shows 4 queries; <code>test</code> on the controller spec passes.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Mail inbox -->
    <section id="mail-inbox" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-cyan-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-cyan-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M3 8l7.89 5.26a2 2 0 002.22 0L21 8M5 19h14a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Mail inbox
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            With <code class="text-cyan-400">soli serve --dev</code>, open <code class="text-amber-400">/__soli/inbox</code> &mdash; or click <code class="text-orange-400">tools</code> in the dev bar &mdash; to read every email the app has sent since the server started &mdash; a built-in MailCatcher, with no second process to run. Each message opens with its headers, its attachments, and tabs for the <strong class="text-white">HTML</strong> body (sandboxed iframe), the <strong class="text-white">text</strong> part, and the <strong class="text-white">raw</strong> RFC 5322 source, downloadable as <code class="text-cyan-400">.eml</code>. The listing is searchable (<code>?q=</code> matches subjects, addresses, bodies and attachment names) and paginated (<code>?per=</code> / <code>?page=</code>).
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <strong class="text-white">No local SMTP server required</strong> &mdash; under <code>--dev</code> a mailer with no configured host captures into the inbox instead of failing, so a signup flow works on a laptop with no mail infrastructure. Messages are tagged <code class="text-green-400">sent</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">captured</code>, or <code class="text-red-400">failed</code> (with the error), and failures are captured too. See the <a href="/docs/builtins/mailer#inbox" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Mailer docs</a> for the full tour, and <code class="text-amber-400">/__soli/mailers</code> to preview templates with fake data instead.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <p class="text-amber-300 text-sm">
                <strong>Dev-only.</strong> The inbox is in-memory (last 100 messages, cleared on restart) and the routes exist solely under <code>--dev</code>. Nothing is captured in production.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Development Mode -->
    <section id="development-mode" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10.325 4.317c.426-1.756 2.924-1.756 3.35 0a1.724 1.724 0 002.573 1.066c1.543-.94 3.31.826 2.37 2.37a1.724 1.724 0 001.065 2.572c1.756.426 1.756 2.924 0 3.35a1.724 1.724 0 00-1.066 2.573c.94 1.543-.826 3.31-2.37 2.37a1.724 1.724 0 00-2.572 1.065c-.426 1.756-2.924 1.756-3.35 0a1.724 1.724 0 00-2.573-1.066c-1.543.94-3.31-.826-2.37-2.37a1.724 1.724 0 00-1.065-2.572c-1.756-.426-1.756-2.924 0-3.35a1.724 1.724 0 001.066-2.573c-.94-1.543.826-3.31 2.37-2.37.996.608 2.296.07 2.572-1.065z" />
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 12a3 3 0 11-6 0 3 3 0 016 0z" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Development Mode
        </h2>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Start the development server with hot reload to enable all debugging features:
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm"># Start development server with hot reload
soli serve

# Allow the debug REPL from another trusted local machine
# (SEC-051: the secret is required — startup refuses without it)
SOLI_DEV_REPL_ALLOW_REMOTE=1 SOLI_DEV_REPL_SECRET=&lt;long-random-string&gt; soli serve . --dev

# The server will automatically reload when files change
# Breakpoints and debug page work in this mode</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Development vs Production</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Feature</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Development</th>
                        <th class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400 font-medium">Production</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Breakpoints</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">The debug page (200)</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-red-400">The request fails (500)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Debug Page</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">Full interactive</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-red-400">Simple error</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Hot Reload</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">Enabled</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-red-400">Disabled</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">Stack Traces</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">Detailed</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-amber-400">Minimal</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400">AQL Query Log (<code>dev_queries()</code>)</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">Captured per request</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-red-400">Always returns []</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-4 text-gray-400"><code>dev_mode?()</code></td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-emerald-400">true</td><td class="py-2 px-4 text-red-400">false</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-cyan-400">dev_mode?()</code> is how the app asks which of the two it is &mdash; to serve a screen only while it is being built, for instance. It reads the <code class="text-cyan-400">--dev</code> flag, not an environment variable, so a <code>.env</code> copied to production cannot turn it on. <code class="text-cyan-400">soli test</code> runs its server without <code>--dev</code>, so it answers <code>false</code> in specs.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-red-500/10 border border-red-500/20 p-4 mb-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-red-400 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z" />
                </svg>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-red-400 font-semibold mb-1">Security Warning</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-300">
                        Never run in development mode in production. The debug page exposes sensitive information including request data, environment variables, and source code. Only set <code>SOLI_DEV_REPL_ALLOW_REMOTE=1</code> on trusted local networks, and always pair it with <code>SOLI_DEV_REPL_SECRET</code> so the REPL credential isn't embedded in HTML error pages (SEC-051).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action -->
    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-500/10 via-orange-500/10 to-orange-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 p-8 text-center">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Next Steps</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Combine debugging with testing to build robust applications.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
            <a href="/docs/testing" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-amber-600 hover:bg-amber-500 text-white font-medium transition-colors">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
                </svg>
                Testing Guide
            </a>
            <a href="/docs/live-reload" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-white/5 hover:bg-white/10 text-white font-medium transition-colors border border-white/10">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4 4v5h.582m15.356 2A8.001 8.001 0 004.582 9m0 0H9m11 11v-5h-.581m0 0a8.003 8.003 0 01-15.357-2m15.357 2H15" />
                </svg>
                Live Reload
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Errors on the command line -->
    <section id="command-line-errors" class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Errors on the Command Line</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Outside the server, an error prints its usual line and a <strong class="text-white">code frame</strong> under it &mdash;
            the file, the source line, a caret, the &ldquo;did you mean&rdquo; suggestion and a stable error code that
            <code>soli explain</code> describes:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-text text-sm">Error: Type error: Undefined variable 'nme' (did you mean 'name'?) at 2:7
 --&gt; report.sl:2:7
  |
2 | print(nme)
  |       ^^^
  |
  = help: did you mean 'name'?
  = note: error[E0301] undefined-variable — `soli explain E0301`</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            When the error is about something declared in the same file, the frame shows that place too, under a
            <code>:::</code> line and a <code>---</code> underline &mdash; the <code>def</code> a call gives the wrong
            number of arguments to, the class that has no such member:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-text text-sm">Error: Type error: Wrong number of arguments: expected 2, got 3 at 11:1
  --&gt; report.sl:11:1
   |
11 | greet("a", "b", "c")
   | ^^^^^
  ::: report.sl:7:1
   |
 7 | def greet(name, greeting = "Hi")
   | --- declared here
   |
   = note: error[E0403] wrong-arity — `soli explain E0403`</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400">
            See <a href="/docs/development-tools/error-codes" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300 underline">Error Messages &amp; Codes</a>
            for how to read a frame, every code, and the <code>--format json</code> output of <code>soli check</code> and <code>soli lint</code>.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
